            "body": "Mr. Mallick apne bhatije Taksh ko saath lekar Mumbai se Delhi jaa rahe the. Taksh ki ye pehli hawai yatra thi. Check-in counter par Mr. Mallick ne bag diya aur counter wale ne bag par ek lambi si paper-tag chipka di. Taksh ne ghoom kar tag padhne ki koshish ki: usme bada-bada likha tha **DEL**.\n\n\"Mallick uncle, hum to **Delhi** jaa rahe hain. Ye tag par 'Delhi' poora kyun nahi likha? Sirf teen akshar?\"\n\nMr. Mallick hanse. \"Aage dekho.\" Conveyor belt par aate hue doosre bagon par bhi tag the: **BOM**, **BLR**, **MAA**. Taksh ne ginti ki.\n\n\"BLR to Bengaluru hoga. Par **BOM**? Mumbai ka B to hai, par O aur M kahan se aaye? Aur **MAA**? Ye to Chennai hai, isme C, H, E kuch bhi nahi hai!\"\n\n\"Wahi to maza hai,\" Mr. Mallick ne kaha. \"BOM isliye kyunki Mumbai ka purana naam **Bombay** tha. MAA isliye kyunki Chennai ka purana naam **Madras** tha. Airport ke code purane naam se bane, par ab poori duniya mein ek hi code chalta hai. Ek bag Japan mein ho ya Germany mein, 'DEL' padhte hi har kisi ko pata chal jaata hai ki bag Delhi jaayega. Naam lamba nahi, bhasha ka jhanjhat nahi, galti ka darr kam.\"\n\nTaksh ne thodi der socha. \"Matlab chhota naam, par poori duniya ke liye ek jaisa.\"\n\n\"Bilkul. Chemistry mein bhi bilkul yahi hua hai. Har tatva ka ek chhota 'tag' hai, jise **symbol** kehte hain. Aur kuch tatvon ka symbol bhi bilkul **MAA** jaisa hai: naam kuch aur, par symbol purane naam se bana. Chalo, aaj wahi sab samajhte hain.\"\n\n> **Figure 1.1** – Airport tag aur tatva ka tag, dono ek hi sochi-samjhi tarkeeb hai. Dono chhote hain, poori duniya mein ek jaise hain, aur kabhi-kabhi purane naam se bane hain (MAA se Madras, Na se Natrium).\n\n```\n  Shehar                        Airport tag\n  ------------------------------------------\n  Mumbai (pehle Bombay)    ->   [ BOM ]\n  Delhi                    ->   [ DEL ]\n  Bengaluru                ->   [ BLR ]\n  Chennai (pehle Madras)   ->   [ MAA ]\n\n  Tatva                         Chemistry tag (symbol)\n  ------------------------------------------\n  Hydrogen                 ->   [ H  ]\n  Magnesium                ->   [ Mg ]\n  Sodium (Latin: Natrium)  ->   [ Na ]\n  Iron (Latin: Ferrum)     ->   [ Fe ]\n```",
