            "heading": "Part C: Symbol likhne ke niyam",
            "headingHinglish": "Part C: Symbol likhne ke niyam",
            "body": "### Niyam 1: Pehla akshar hamesha CAPITAL\n\nSymbol mein pehla akshar hamesha **bada (capital)** hota hai.\n\n- Hydrogen: **H**\n- Carbon: **C**\n- Oxygen: **O**\n\n### Niyam 2: Doosra akshar (agar ho) hamesha small\n\nKai tatvon ke naam ka pehla akshar doosre tatva ke saath takra jaata hai (jaise Carbon, Calcium, Chlorine, Copper, sabka pehla akshar C hai). Isliye doosre tatvon ke liye **do akshar** liye jaate hain, aur doosra akshar **hamesha chhota (small)** likhte hain.\n\n- Calcium: **Ca** (C capital, a small)\n- Chlorine: **Cl** (C capital, l small)\n- Magnesium: **Mg**\n\n### Niyam 3: Kabhi do capital nahi\n\n**CA**, **MG**, **NA** likhna galat hai. Ye symbols hain hi nahi ya kuch aur matlab de dete hain (aage dekhenge).\n\n> **Figure 1.2** – Symbol ko padhne aur likhne ka tareeka. Pehla akshar capital, doosra (agar ho) small. Do capital letter ek saath likhne ka matlab alag-alag tatva ho jaata hai.\n\n```\n   Ca                              CA  (galat)\n   ^^                              ^^\n   Capital + small                 Do capital: Ca ki jagah\n   = ek tatva (calcium)            ye Calcium ka symbol hi\n                                   nahi hai\n\n   Cl   (chlorine)                 CL, cl, cL  (sab galat)\n   C aur l: ek jodi                Capital aur small ka\n   milkar ek tatva                 niyam toot gaya\n```\n\n### Niyam 4: Symbol ke baad full stop nahi lagta\n\n\"H.\" ya \"Na.\" nahi likhte. Bas **H**, **Na**.",
