          {
            "heading": "Practice Questions",
            "headingHinglish": "Practice Questions",
            "body": "1. What is the atomic number of an element? What does it tell us about the atom, and why is it called the \"identity\" of an element?\n\n2. Define mass number. Write the formula for the number of neutrons.\n\n3. Write in the Z and A form (like ²³₁₁Na) and find the protons, neutrons and electrons of a neutral atom of: (a) carbon (Z = 6, A = 12), (b) oxygen (Z = 8, A = 16), (c) aluminium (Z = 13, A = 27), (d) calcium (Z = 20, A = 40).\n\n4. A neutral atom has 17 protons and 18 neutrons. Find its atomic number, mass number and name the element.\n\n5. An atom has mass number 31 and 16 neutrons. Find Z and the element, and the number of electrons in the neutral atom.\n\n6. Find protons, neutrons and electrons in: (a) Na⁺ (A = 23, Z = 11), (b) Cl⁻ (A = 35, Z = 17), (c) Mg²⁺ (A = 24, Z = 12), (d) O²⁻ (A = 16, Z = 8), (e) Al³⁺ (A = 27, Z = 13).\n\n7. A student says \"Mg²⁺ has 14 electrons because it gained 2\". Find the error and give the correct count.\n\n8. Why do Na⁺, Mg²⁺, O²⁻ and Al³⁺ all have 10 electrons even though they are different elements? What is the difference between them in terms of protons?\n\n9. Argon has mass number 40 and atomic number 18. Potassium has mass number 39 and atomic number 19. In which order should they be placed, and why?\n\n10. State True or False, and correct the false ones:\n    (a) Atomic number equals the number of neutrons.\n    (b) In a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the number of protons.\n    (c) Mass number is always a whole number.\n    (d) When an atom forms an ion, its number of protons changes.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Atomic number Z = nucleus mein **protons ki ginti**. Har tatva ke atom mein protons ki ginti alag aur fixed hoti hai (H mein 1, C mein 6, Na mein 11), isliye Z se hi pata chalta hai ki atom kaunse tatva ka hai. Isiliye ise tatva ki **pehchaan** kehte hain.\n\n2. Mass number A = protons + neutrons (nucleus ke saare kanon ki ginti). Neutrons = **A − Z**.\n\n3. (a) ¹²₆C: protons **6**, neutrons 12 − 6 = **6**, electrons **6**. (b) ¹⁶₈O: protons **8**, neutrons 16 − 8 = **8**, electrons **8**. (c) ²⁷₁₃Al: protons **13**, neutrons 27 − 13 = **14**, electrons **13**. (d) ⁴⁰₂₀Ca: protons **20**, neutrons 40 − 20 = **20**, electrons **20**. Jaanch (protons + neutrons = A): 12, 16, 27, 40 ✅\n\n4. Z = protons = **17**. A = 17 + 18 = **35**. Z = 17 wala tatva **chlorine** hai (³⁵₁₇Cl). Jaanch: 17 + 18 = 35 ✅.\n\n5. Z = A − neutrons = 31 − 16 = **15**. Tatva **phosphorus** hai (³¹₁₅P). Neutral atom mein electrons = **15**. Jaanch: 15 + 16 = 31 ✅.\n\n6. (a) Na⁺: protons **11**, neutrons **12**, electrons 11 − 1 = **10**. (b) Cl⁻: protons **17**, neutrons **18**, electrons 17 + 1 = **18**. (c) Mg²⁺: protons **12**, neutrons **12**, electrons 12 − 2 = **10**. (d) O²⁻: protons **8**, neutrons **8**, electrons 8 + 2 = **10**. (e) Al³⁺: protons **13**, neutrons **14**, electrons 13 − 3 = **10**. Jaanch (charge = protons − electrons): +1, −1, +2, −2, +3 ✅\n\n7. Galti ye hai ki positive charge electrons **khone** se aata hai, paane se nahi. Mg ke neutral atom mein 12 electrons hote hain; Mg²⁺ mein 2 electrons chale gaye, to **12 − 2 = 10** electrons bache. Jaanch: charge = 12 − 10 = +2 ✅.\n\n8. Chaaron ion mein electrons ki ginti 10 hai, par unke nucleus alag hain: Na⁺ mein 11 protons, Mg²⁺ mein 12, O²⁻ mein 8, Al³⁺ mein 13. Tatva protons se tay hota hai, electrons se nahi, isliye ye chaar alag tatva hain. (Electrons ke 10 hone ka asli reason Topic 5 mein aayega.)\n\n9. Order Z se tay hota hai: pehle **argon (Z = 18)**, phir **potassium (Z = 19)**. Mass number se argon (40) potassium (39) ke baad aata, jo galat hota. Z hi tatva ki asli pehchaan hai, isliye Kram usi se banta hai.\n\n10. (a) **False** ❌. Atomic number protons ki ginti hai; neutrons = A − Z. (b) **True** ✅. (c) **True** ✅. (d) **False** ❌. Ion banne par sirf electrons badalte hain, protons nahi.\n\n---\n\nShabaash! Ab tum Ishwar ki tarah samajh gaye ho ki roll number (Z) pehchaan hai aur class ka total (A) ginti. Kisi bhi atom ya ion ke protons, neutrons aur electrons ab tum seconds mein bata sakte ho.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ek chhota sa mazedaar sawaal baaki hai. Na⁺, Mg²⁺, O²⁻ aur Al³⁺ mein electrons 10 hi kyun hain? Aur Chapter 3 ki valency table mein Na ki valency 1, Mg ki 2 aur Al ki 3 kyun thi? Iska raaz electrons ke **shells mein baithne ke tareeke** mein chhupa hai. Agle topic **\"Electronic Configuration aur Valency\"** mein hum pehle 20 tatvon ke shells bharenge aur ye sab saaf ho jaayega.",
            "bodyHinglish": "1. What is the atomic number of an element? What does it tell us about the atom, and why is it called the \"identity\" of an element?\n\n2. Define mass number. Write the formula for the number of neutrons.\n\n3. Write in the Z and A form (like ²³₁₁Na) and find the protons, neutrons and electrons of a neutral atom of: (a) carbon (Z = 6, A = 12), (b) oxygen (Z = 8, A = 16), (c) aluminium (Z = 13, A = 27), (d) calcium (Z = 20, A = 40).\n\n4. A neutral atom has 17 protons and 18 neutrons. Find its atomic number, mass number and name the element.\n\n5. An atom has mass number 31 and 16 neutrons. Find Z and the element, and the number of electrons in the neutral atom.\n\n6. Find protons, neutrons and electrons in: (a) Na⁺ (A = 23, Z = 11), (b) Cl⁻ (A = 35, Z = 17), (c) Mg²⁺ (A = 24, Z = 12), (d) O²⁻ (A = 16, Z = 8), (e) Al³⁺ (A = 27, Z = 13).\n\n7. A student says \"Mg²⁺ has 14 electrons because it gained 2\". Find the error and give the correct count.\n\n8. Why do Na⁺, Mg²⁺, O²⁻ and Al³⁺ all have 10 electrons even though they are different elements? What is the difference between them in terms of protons?\n\n9. Argon has mass number 40 and atomic number 18. Potassium has mass number 39 and atomic number 19. In which order should they be placed, and why?\n\n10. State True or False, and correct the false ones:\n    (a) Atomic number equals the number of neutrons.\n    (b) In a neutral atom, the number of electrons equals the number of protons.\n    (c) Mass number is always a whole number.\n    (d) When an atom forms an ion, its number of protons changes.\n\n### Answers\n\n1. Atomic number Z = nucleus mein **protons ki ginti**. Har tatva ke atom mein protons ki ginti alag aur fixed hoti hai (H mein 1, C mein 6, Na mein 11), isliye Z se hi pata chalta hai ki atom kaunse tatva ka hai. Isiliye ise tatva ki **pehchaan** kehte hain.\n\n2. Mass number A = protons + neutrons (nucleus ke saare kanon ki ginti). Neutrons = **A − Z**.\n\n3. (a) ¹²₆C: protons **6**, neutrons 12 − 6 = **6**, electrons **6**. (b) ¹⁶₈O: protons **8**, neutrons 16 − 8 = **8**, electrons **8**. (c) ²⁷₁₃Al: protons **13**, neutrons 27 − 13 = **14**, electrons **13**. (d) ⁴⁰₂₀Ca: protons **20**, neutrons 40 − 20 = **20**, electrons **20**. Jaanch (protons + neutrons = A): 12, 16, 27, 40 ✅\n\n4. Z = protons = **17**. A = 17 + 18 = **35**. Z = 17 wala tatva **chlorine** hai (³⁵₁₇Cl). Jaanch: 17 + 18 = 35 ✅.\n\n5. Z = A − neutrons = 31 − 16 = **15**. Tatva **phosphorus** hai (³¹₁₅P). Neutral atom mein electrons = **15**. Jaanch: 15 + 16 = 31 ✅.\n\n6. (a) Na⁺: protons **11**, neutrons **12**, electrons 11 − 1 = **10**. (b) Cl⁻: protons **17**, neutrons **18**, electrons 17 + 1 = **18**. (c) Mg²⁺: protons **12**, neutrons **12**, electrons 12 − 2 = **10**. (d) O²⁻: protons **8**, neutrons **8**, electrons 8 + 2 = **10**. (e) Al³⁺: protons **13**, neutrons **14**, electrons 13 − 3 = **10**. Jaanch (charge = protons − electrons): +1, −1, +2, −2, +3 ✅\n\n7. Galti ye hai ki positive charge electrons **khone** se aata hai, paane se nahi. Mg ke neutral atom mein 12 electrons hote hain; Mg²⁺ mein 2 electrons chale gaye, to **12 − 2 = 10** electrons bache. Jaanch: charge = 12 − 10 = +2 ✅.\n\n8. Chaaron ion mein electrons ki ginti 10 hai, par unke nucleus alag hain: Na⁺ mein 11 protons, Mg²⁺ mein 12, O²⁻ mein 8, Al³⁺ mein 13. Tatva protons se tay hota hai, electrons se nahi, isliye ye chaar alag tatva hain. (Electrons ke 10 hone ka asli reason Topic 5 mein aayega.)\n\n9. Order Z se tay hota hai: pehle **argon (Z = 18)**, phir **potassium (Z = 19)**. Mass number se argon (40) potassium (39) ke baad aata, jo galat hota. Z hi tatva ki asli pehchaan hai, isliye Kram usi se banta hai.\n\n10. (a) **False** ❌. Atomic number protons ki ginti hai; neutrons = A − Z. (b) **True** ✅. (c) **True** ✅. (d) **False** ❌. Ion banne par sirf electrons badalte hain, protons nahi.\n\n---\n\nShabaash! Ab tum Ishwar ki tarah samajh gaye ho ki roll number (Z) pehchaan hai aur class ka total (A) ginti. Kisi bhi atom ya ion ke protons, neutrons aur electrons ab tum seconds mein bata sakte ho.\n\n**Aage kya?** Ek chhota sa mazedaar sawaal baaki hai. Na⁺, Mg²⁺, O²⁻ aur Al³⁺ mein electrons 10 hi kyun hain? Aur Chapter 3 ki valency table mein Na ki valency 1, Mg ki 2 aur Al ki 3 kyun thi? Iska raaz electrons ke **shells mein baithne ke tareeke** mein chhupa hai. Agle topic **\"Electronic Configuration aur Valency\"** mein hum pehle 20 tatvon ke shells bharenge aur ye sab saaf ho jaayega."
          }
